#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# bezier_curve

#% text
# `bezier_curve([p0, p1, p2, p3, ...])` is a graphics primitive: a composite cubic Bézier curve from p0 through p3, p6, ..., with the points between them as control points.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Each piece is cubic: p0 to p3 with controls p1 and p2, then p3 to p6 with controls p4 and p5, and so on.

#% item
# Two points left at the end make a quadratic piece, one point a straight segment.

#% item
# The curve passes through its end points and is tangent there to the lines towards the neighbouring control points.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# One cubic piece:

#% cell
graphics(bezier_curve([[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 1], [1, 0]]))
#% output
#= image("bezier_curve.files/bezier_curve-1.svgz")

#% text
# The curve with its control polygon, dashed:

#% cell
graphics([bezier_curve([[0, 0], [1, 2], [2, -1], [3, 1]]), gray, dashing(dashed), line([[0, 0], [1, 2], [2, -1], [3, 1]])])
#% output
#= image("bezier_curve.files/bezier_curve-2.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Two pieces joined smoothly at [2, 0]:

#% cell
graphics([red, bezier_curve([[0, 0], [1, 1], [1.5, 0], [2, 0], [2.5, 0], [3, -1], [4, 0]])])
#% output
#= image("bezier_curve.files/bezier_curve-3.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `line`, `circle`, `graphics`
